items.statement:
Assume Countable Choice. In the symmetric case of [[def-ltwo-operator-associated-with-a-symmetric-elliptic-form]], let $\Omega\subseteq\mathbb R^n$ be open, fix $\mu\ge\beta$ with $\beta$ as in [[thm-garding-inequality-for-a-divergence-form-elliptic-operator]], and let $K_\mu$ be the shifted solution operator of [[def-shifted-elliptic-solution-operator]]. Then:
1. $D(L)$ is dense in $L^2(\Omega)$;
2. $L$ is symmetric, i.e. $(Lu,v)_{L^2}=(u,Lv)_{L^2}$ for all $u,v\in D(L)$;
3. $L$ is lower bounded, i.e. $(Lu,u)_{L^2}=a(u,u)\ge-\beta\|u\|_{L^2}^2$ for every $u\in D(L)$ (and also $a(u,u)\ge-\mu\|u\|_{L^2}^2$).
No boundary regularity of $\Omega$ is used.

items.strategy:
The family $\{\sqrt{
u_j}e_j\}$, with $
u_j=(\lambda_j+\mu)^{-1}$, is orthonormal for the inner product $a_\mu$; completeness holds because $a_\mu(u,e_j)=(\lambda_j+\mu)(u,e_j)_{L^2}$ (test the weak eigen-equation with $u$ and use symmetry and the reality of $\lambda_j$), so a $u$ that is $a_\mu$-orthogonal to every $\sqrt{
u_j}e_j$ satisfies $(u,e_j)_{L^2}=0$ for all $j$ and hence $u=0$ in $L^2$ by the completeness in [[thm-discrete-spectrum-of-a-symmetric-elliptic-dirichlet-operator]]. The Hilbert-space Fourier expansion and Parseval in $(H^1_0,a_\mu)$ give claim 1, Parseval in $L^2$ gives claim 2, and subtracting $\mu$ times claim 2 from claim 1 gives claim 3; Bessel's inequality ensures the sums are finite ([L] Chapter 4, Section 4.1, end of the proof of Theorem 4.2 and Remark 4.3, printed p. 86; [B] Remark 28 after Theorem 9.31, printed pp. 311-312).

items.statement:
Statement refuted: every eigenvalue of the Dirichlet Laplacian on a bounded domain has one-dimensional eigenspace, so the eigenvalues listed with multiplicity have no repetitions. On the square $\Omega=(0,\pi)^2$, let $u(x,y)=\sin x\,\sin 2y$ and $v(x,y)=\sin 2x\,\sin y$. Both belong to $H^1_0(\Omega)$: multiplying each by smooth cutoffs supported away from the four sides gives a sequence in $C_c^\infty(\Omega)$ converging to it in $H^1(\Omega)$. Direct integration by parts gives $\int_\Omega
abla u\cdot\overline{
abla\varphi}=5\int_\Omega u\overline\varphi$ and $\int_\Omega
abla v\cdot\overline{
abla\varphi}=5\int_\Omega v\overline\varphi$ for every $\varphi\in C_c^\infty(\Omega)$; density and continuity extend both identities to every $\varphi\in H^1_0(\Omega)$. Thus $u$ and $v$ are weak Dirichlet eigenfunctions with common eigenvalue $5=1^2+2^2=2^2+1^2$ ([[def-symmetric-elliptic-weak-eigenpair]], [[def-wkp-zero-as-a-sobolev-closure]]). They are nonzero and $L^2$-orthogonal, since their inner product factors into $\int_0^\pi\sin x\sin 2x\,dx=0$ and $\int_0^\pi\sin 2y\sin y\,dy=0$. Hence they are linearly independent and the eigenspace at $5$ has dimension at least two. The discrete spectral theorem and Courant--Fischer formula therefore list this eigenvalue with multiplicity, rather than as a simple eigenvalue.
